  "contexts": [
    {
      "id": "set-03-case-1",
      "title": "Northwind Health — regulated container platform",
      "body": "Northwind Health runs a patient-facing intake application and several internal batch jobs on Azure Kubernetes Service, with images stored in Azure Container Registry.\n\nA recent audit produced three findings. First, deployments reference image tags such as api:latest, and the audit could not prove which build was running in production during an incident. Second, several pods read a database password from a Kubernetes Secret that was committed to the deployment repository. Third, container images are pulled over the public internet, and the auditor requires that registry traffic never traverse it.\n\nThe intake workload is bursty: near zero overnight, then a sharp spike each morning. The batch jobs are interruptible and cost is a concern. The platform team also needs each workload to hold only the Azure permissions it actually needs, and must be able to prove which identity performed a given action.",
      "requirements": [
        "Provably immutable, verifiable image references in production",
        "No long-lived secrets in the cluster or in source control",
        "Registry traffic must not traverse the public internet",
        "Scale to near zero overnight, absorb a morning spike",
        "Reduce cost for interruptible batch workloads",
        "Per-workload Azure identity with least privilege and an audit trail"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "set-06-case-1",
      "title": "Contoso Retail — semantic product search",
      "body": "Contoso Retail is replacing keyword product search with a semantic search service for a catalogue of 4 million SKUs.\n\nThe ingestion pipeline embeds each product description with a 1536-dimension model and writes the vector alongside the product record. Catalogue updates arrive continuously from three regional merchandising teams, and roughly 40,000 products change on a normal weekday.\n\nThe storefront queries the service on every keystroke after a 200 ms debounce. Peak traffic is 12,000 queries per second on promotional days, and 95% of those queries hit the same 2,000 popular products. Product records are read far more often than they are written.\n\nThe search API must return results in under 150 ms at the 99th percentile. Search is regarded as revenue-critical: a regional outage must not take the storefront down.",
      "requirements": [
        "Vector similarity search over 4 million embeddings",
        "Sub-150 ms p99 read latency for the storefront",
        "Continuous ingestion without blocking reads",
        "Survive the loss of a single Azure region",
        "Control cost — the team flagged RU consumption as a concern"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "set-10-case-1",
      "title": "Fabrikam Logistics — order and telemetry platform",
      "body": "Fabrikam Logistics runs an order platform on Azure Container Apps, split into an order API, a billing worker, and a notification service.\n\nWhen an order is placed the API must write the order to its database and publish an OrderPlaced event. Billing must process the events for a given customer strictly in the order they were raised, because a later amendment may reverse an earlier charge. Billing is idempotent by design but has been observed to charge twice during redeploys.\n\nSeparately, 250,000 delivery vehicles emit GPS and sensor telemetry every 5 seconds. Two independent teams consume this stream: the live tracking map, and an analytics job that must be able to reprocess the previous three days after a model change.\n\nThe notification service calls a third-party SMS gateway that is rate limited to 10 requests per second and returns HTTP 429 beyond it. During promotional bursts the notification service has been overwhelming the gateway.",
      "requirements": [
        "No order may be published without its database write succeeding",
        "Per-customer ordering for billing events",
        "Exactly-once financial effect despite at-least-once delivery",
        "Two independent consumers of the telemetry stream, with 3-day replay",
        "Respect the downstream 10 requests/second SMS limit"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "set-13-case-1",
      "title": "Tailwind Traders — secrets, identity, and observability",
      "body": "Tailwind Traders operates a multi-tenant SaaS platform across App Service, Azure Functions, and AKS.\n\nSecrets are currently a single Key Vault shared by every application and environment, and each service authenticates with a client secret stored in app settings. A recent near-miss saw a leaked secret grant access to production storage. Security has mandated that no application store a credential of any kind, and that a compromise of one workload must not expose another's secrets.\n\nThe database password must rotate every 60 days with zero downtime. An earlier attempt caused an outage because applications cached the old value and kept presenting a revoked credential.\n\nOn the observability side, one Application Insights resource is shared by all services, and during incidents the team cannot tell which service is failing or follow a request across service boundaries. Telemetry ingestion cost has also tripled year on year, and a daily cap was applied that silently created gaps in the data.",
      "requirements": [
        "No stored credentials in any application",
        "A compromised workload must not reach another workload's secrets",
        "60-day password rotation with no failed authentications",
        "Attribute telemetry to a specific service and follow a request end to end",
        "Reduce ingestion cost without losing representative data or creating gaps"
      ]
    }
  ],
